{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfbetornffBRrUgeKkrRx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've rewritten the repair candidate and it passes the self-check (`OK`). Novelty against the other models isn't checked by that command, so admission could still reject it.\n\n**What changed.** The rejected attempt took the rank-1 model and added the best model's run-length variety term. That combination predicts almost exactly like `run_variety_asymmetric_ideal_rate` (RMSE 0.0003). This time I refined `length_load_encoding_noise` (rank 2) instead. That model makes the gap in how random the two sequences look decide the choice more or less sharply depending on sequence length, through one shared exponent. The one change is the same variety term grafted onto it: sequences whose runs all have the same length look designed, and sequences with runs of different lengths look random, by one shared weight.\n\n**Why it should pass the novelty gate.** In that model's existing fit the length exponent is about −0.43, meaning longer pairs are decided more sharply. On the novelty pool (lengths 4–8) that makes the same impression gap count about 0.82× as much at length 4 and 1.22× at length 8. The best model has no length term at all, so it can't produce that pattern. The risk is that if the new fit pushes the exponent near zero, the model collapses onto the incumbent `run_length_variety_preference` and gets rejected again.\n\n**Self-check result.** The smoke fit's ELPD-LOO was −2268.8. That is only a rough figure: it came from one short chain (100 draws), so convergence wasn't verified. PSIS-LOO was flagged unreliable on that fit, with 7.1% of trials over the Pareto-k threshold against a 1% tolerance. The full fit at admission will show whether that persists.\n\nFiles are in `iter_4/candidate_5_repair_1/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt` (`length_scaled_run_variety_load`)\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"s